   "A": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the election law, the labor law, and the education law, in relation to authorizing persons seventeen years of age to serve as election inspectors and poll clerks and to repeal section 3207-a of the education law relating to authorizing the board of education of the city of New York to reduce the length of recitation periods",
    "basePrintNo": "A4467",
    "basePrintNoStr": "A4467-2009",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [
      {
       "alternate": false,
       "chamber": "ASSEMBLY",
       "districtCode": 74,
       "fullName": "Brian Kavanagh",
       "imgName": "526_brian_kavanagh.jpg",
       "incumbent": false,
       "memberId": 526,
       "sessionMemberId": 214,
       "sessionYear": 2009,
       "shortName": "KAVANAGH"
      }
     ],
     "size": 1
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §3-400, El L; amd §§132 & 143, Lab L; amd §§2025, 2036 & 2607, rpld & add §3207-a, Ed L",
    "lawSection": "Election Law",
    "memo": "BILL NUMBER:A4467A\n\nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to amend the election law, the labor law, and the\neducation law, in relation to authorizing persons seventeen years of age\nto serve as election inspectors and poll clerks and to repeal section\n3207-a of the education law relating to authorizing the board of educa-\ntion of the city of New York to reduce the length of recitation periods\n\nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA: To provide the authority for enrolled students\nat least 17 years of age, upon consent from their school district and\nparents (or legal guardians), to serve as election inspectors and poll\nclerks during elections.\n\nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS: Amends subdivision 6 of section 3-400\nand adds a new subdivision 7 to the election law to allow a person who\nis 17 years of age who is properly enrolled in a school district to be\neligible to be appointed as an election inspector or poll clerk.\n\nAmends sections 132 and 143 of the labor law to exempt those participat-\ning as election inspectors or poll clerks on election day from the labor\nlaw which limits 17 year olds from working over 4 hours a day on a\nschool day and exempts participants from needing working papers.\n\nAdds a new section 2036 to the education law providing the authority for\nschool districts to allow 17 year old students to participate as\nelection inspectors and poll clerks at school district elections.\n\nAdds a new section 3207-a to the education law to allow 17 year olds to\nparticipate as election inspectors and poll clerks, upon parental\nconsent. Makes clear that those students participating as election\ninspectors or poll clerks shall be deemed as attending school on\nelection days.\n\nThe bill makes a technical change to the education law by repealing\nsection 3207-a of the education law that expired in 1959 and was never\nextended.\n\nJUSTIFICATION: Recruitment of election inspectors and poll clerks to\nwork our elections is a difficult task. For every statewide election, 4,\nelection inspectors or poll clerks are required per election district\npolling station: roughly 60,000 people across the state to work the\nelection. In the November 2001 election, severe shortages of personnel\nto work the election existed in several areas of the State.  This legis-\nlation would help reduce such widespread shortages while providing an\nexcellent hands-on learning experience of democracy in action for our\nstate's young adults.\n\nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY: A.11173 (2002), Al252 (2003-04), A3006\n(2005-06). A.4059 (2007-2008).\n\nFISCAL IMPLICATION FOR STATE AND LOCAL GOVERNMENTS:  None.\n\nEFFECTIVE DATE: This act shall take effect immediately\n\n",
